      <h1 class="art-title">Stripchat's Account Holder Rule: <span class="art-grad">Who Gets Paid on a Group Account</span></h1>
      <p class="art-lead">If a Stripchat account has two or more registered people on it, one of them must be named the Account Holder: the person who is present at every broadcast and receives every payout. The rule is simple to state and easy to get wrong, especially for duos, small teams and studios that register accounts casually.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="art-body container">
    <div class="art-layout">
      <aside class="art-toc">
        <p class="art-toc__title">Contents</p>
        <ol class="art-toc__list">
          <li><a href="#what-it-says">What the Rule Says</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who">Who It Applies To</a></li>
          <li><a href="#money">Where the Money Goes</a></li>
          <li><a href="#presence">Present at Every Broadcast</a></li>
          <li><a href="#setup">New vs. Existing Accounts</a></li>
          <li><a href="#studios">For Studios: A Group-Account Audit</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
        </ol>
        <a href="#cta" class="art-toc__cta">Get help →</a>
      </aside>

      <main class="art-main">

      <p class="art-intro">Group accounts are where platform rules meet real-world messiness: couples who stream together, friends sharing a room, studio setups where several people rotate through one profile. The Account Holder rule, announced in 2024 and still in force, is Stripchat's answer to a plain question: who is responsible for this account, and who gets the money? Here is what it says, what it changes in practice, and how studios should structure accounts around it.</p>
      <p class="card-date" style="margin:0 0 1.75rem;">EWOhub Editorial Team · Published Sep 20, 2026</p>

      <section class="section" id="what-it-says">
        <h2>What the Rule Actually Says</h2>
        <div class="glass-card" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
          <p>Per Stripchat's announcement (as reported by BCAMS Magazine), any account with two or more registered persons must designate an Account Holder. That person has to be present during every broadcast, and receives all payouts as the designated beneficiary of the chosen payment method.</p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0;">Three things follow from that. There is exactly one holder per account. The role is tied to both presence and money. And nothing in the announcement lets a group split payouts inside Stripchat. Anything beyond the announcement (exact wording, edge cases, current enforcement) lives in Stripchat's own FAQ, which is where to confirm before you restructure anything.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="who">
        <h2>Who It Applies To</h2>

        <div class="two-col-box">
          <div class="two-col-box__item two-col-box__item--good">
            <h3>Covered by the rule</h3>
            <ul class="checklist">
              <li>Accounts with two or more registered persons: couples, duos, small groups</li>
              <li>Existing group accounts, which got a 45-day transition period to name their Account Holder</li>
              <li>New group accounts, where the first registered person becomes the Account Holder automatically</li>
              <li>Studio setups that run one shared profile for several people instead of one profile per person</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="two-col-box__item two-col-box__item--bad">
            <h3>Not what this rule is about</h3>
            <ul class="checklist">
              <li>Solo accounts with a single registered person, where the account owner is the payee anyway</li>
              <li>A studio-level account setup, which has its own onboarding and identity requirements: check Stripchat's studio documentation instead of assuming this rule replaces it</li>
              <li>A way to split earnings between members inside Stripchat: the announcement describes none</li>
              <li>A penalty schedule: the announcement specifies no penalties, which is not a reason to test the rule</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="money">
        <h2>Where the Money Goes</h2>
        <div class="glass-card" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
          <p>All payouts go to the Account Holder, through the payment method they chose. Not to the account name, and not to whoever did the most shows that week: to one person's payout method. From Stripchat's side, that person is the beneficiary.</p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0;">What the members agree between themselves is their own business, not Stripchat's, and that is exactly the risk. If the split lives in a chat message, the first dispute or the first departure will be painful. Anything the holder passes on after being paid is a private arrangement, so put it in writing while everyone is still on good terms.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="presence">
        <h2>The "Present at Every Broadcast" Requirement</h2>

        <div class="reason-card" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
          <div class="reason-num">01</div>
          <div class="reason-content">
            <h3>It is a scheduling constraint, not a formality</h3>
            <p>If the holder must be on every broadcast, a shared profile can't run shifts where other people cover hours without the holder. Build the schedule around the holder's availability, not the other way around.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="reason-card">
          <div class="reason-num">02</div>
          <div class="reason-content">
            <h3>Rotating-cast profiles are the exposed case</h3>
            <p>One account used by several people in turns fits this rule worst, and it is common in studio setups. If that is how you operate, the honest fix is usually one account per person, not one holder who can't realistically be everywhere.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="reason-card">
          <div class="reason-num">03</div>
          <div class="reason-content">
            <h3>Duos are the easy case</h3>
            <p>For a couple who always stream together, the rule mostly means choosing whose payout method and paperwork the money flows through. The presence requirement is met naturally.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="reason-card">
          <div class="reason-num">04</div>
          <div class="reason-content">
            <h3>Write down who the holder is</h3>
            <p>It is a simple record, but it is the one you will want when a payout question comes up: who the holder is, which payment method is attached, and who else is registered on the account.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="setup">
        <h2>Setting It Up: New vs. Existing Accounts</h2>
        <div class="glass-card" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
          <p>For existing group accounts, Stripchat ran a 45-day transition during which the group selected its Account Holder; the end date was shown on the My Documents page. If your group account predates the rule and nobody made that choice, check My Documents and the FAQ instead of assuming it is settled.</p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0;">For new accounts there is no choice to make: the first registered person automatically becomes the Account Holder, and it can't be changed. That makes registration order a real decision. Whoever signs up first ends up as the payee and the person who must be on every stream, so decide who that is before anyone registers, not after.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="studios">
        <div class="highlight-box">
          <h2>For Studios: A Group-Account Audit</h2>
          <p>Studios usually hit this rule sideways: an account opened years ago by whoever was at the desk, now used by three people. A short audit avoids the messy version of that conversation later.</p>
          <ul class="checklist">
              <li>List every account with two or more registered persons and name the holder on each: the person recorded in My Documents, not the person you assume.</li>
              <li>Check that each holder can actually be present at every broadcast on that account. If not, split it into one account per person.</li>
              <li>Confirm the payment method attached to each holder is one that person controls and is comfortable being the recorded beneficiary on.</li>
              <li>Put the internal split, and what happens if the holder leaves, into a signed agreement. The announcement describes the role as fixed on new accounts, so plan turnover as an account change and ask Stripchat support for the process before you need it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0;">Add it to your onboarding checklist next to the <a href="/stripchat-first-14-days-guide">first-14-days guide →</a>, and keep an eye on <a href="/stripchat-model-news-studio-admin">Model News for studio admins →</a> for rule changes.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="faq-section section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-chevron" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>Does every Stripchat account need an Account Holder?</span>
            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none"><path d="M5 7.5l5 5 5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>No. The rule applies to accounts with two or more registered persons. A solo account has one registered person, who is the payee by default.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-chevron" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>Can a group split payouts between members on Stripchat?</span>
            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none"><path d="M5 7.5l5 5 5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>The announcement describes no way to do that: payouts go to the Account Holder as the beneficiary of the chosen payment method. Splitting the money is a private arrangement between members, so document it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-chevron" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>What happens if the Account Holder leaves the group?</span>
            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none"><path d="M5 7.5l5 5 5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>The announcement doesn't cover it. For new accounts the role is described as fixed, so treat a holder leaving as an account change and ask Stripchat support how to handle it, ideally before it happens.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

